# An Airy Spirit — release signing keys

Every release on
[GitHub Releases](https://github.com/Seventhwalllab/anairyspirit/releases)
comes with a `SHA256SUMS` file listing the checksum of each download, and
`SHA256SUMS.asc`, a GPG signature over that list. Check both before you run
anything you downloaded.

## Release key (GPG)

```
UID          Anairyspirit Release <release@seventhwalllab.com>
Fingerprint  009B 027D B44A CA76 C1BE  8C4D 4E4B E48F B42F 8D86
Algorithm    Ed25519
Valid until  2031-05-21
```

The key also carries an older user ID, `Mehengram Test Operator`, marked
revoked. That is expected: the product was renamed, the key stayed the same.

## Check a download

You need `gpg` once: on macOS `brew install gnupg`; on Debian or Ubuntu
`sudo apt-get install gnupg`; on Fedora `sudo dnf install gnupg2`.

In the folder with the files you downloaded, plus `SHA256SUMS` and
`SHA256SUMS.asc` from the same release:

```bash
gpg --import KEYS                       # once: the key block below
gpg --verify SHA256SUMS.asc SHA256SUMS  # expect: Good signature from "Anairyspirit Release <release@seventhwalllab.com>"
sha256sum --check --ignore-missing SHA256SUMS        # Linux
shasum -a 256 --check --ignore-missing SHA256SUMS    # macOS
```

Compare the fingerprint gpg prints with the one above. `BAD signature`, a
different fingerprint, or any line marked `FAILED` means: do not run it —
download again from the release page.

